WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:07.440
Welcome back to Curious Archive. This video is the&nbsp;
second half of a series exploring the biology and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:13.120
ecosystems of realistic Pokémon. The Realistic&nbsp;
Pokémon art project was created by the talented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.720
RJ Palmer, aka ‘Arvalis’ I have links below&nbsp;
where you can follow and support his work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.640
This second round of redesigns features amazing&nbsp;
biological details and an even wider variety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.840
of Pokémon. And please remember the disclaimer&nbsp;
from the first video — these realistic lifeforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:35.520
are an exciting speculative exercise, but not an&nbsp;
attempt to somehow ‘improve’ the stylized designs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.640
of the original games. They’re just fun alternate&nbsp;
takes that imagine Pokémon in something closer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.520
real world anatomy, even if these lifeforms are&nbsp;
more fantastical than what’s likely to appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:51.040
in nature. So, let’s jump into part two of&nbsp;
our nature documentary of another world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:57.360
and return to the astonishing domain of realistic&nbsp;
Pokémon… Beginning with Gen II starters, we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.800
Meganium and its pre-evolutions. The designs of&nbsp;
these creatures take inspiration from sauropods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.440
but also from modern tortoises. And while&nbsp;
tortoises have evolved to eat grasses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:13.040
Palmer imagines the long necks of Meganium help&nbsp;
them reach their preferred food of treetop fungi.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:17.680
And speaking of fungi, the antennae at the end&nbsp;
of Meganium’s nostrils are actually a unique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:22.960
strain of fungi — which Palmer states the titans&nbsp;
have a symbiotic relationship with — although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:27.680
the exact purpose is unknown. In contrast, the&nbsp;
flower-like structure on the front of their shell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:33.920
is not a plant but a brightly colored frill that&nbsp;
mimics flower petals. This might seem strange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:38.880
but animals camouflaging themselves as plants&nbsp;
has been documented numerous times in nature:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.000
like insects whose wings have&nbsp;
evolved to resemble leaves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:48.560
Up next, we have Typhlosion and its pre-evolutions&nbsp;
— reimagined with greater detail and anatomical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:54.240
plausibility. In its claws and fur pattern, this&nbsp;
reimagining resembles a cross between some sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:00.400
badger and a sun bear. Palmer imagines Typhlosion&nbsp;
feeds primarily on Combees hives, and the wax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:05.600
from the hives has a chemical reaction with&nbsp;
Typhlosion saliva to create a flammable substance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.160
Typhlosions then use their tongues to coat their&nbsp;
quills in this chemical, and then they light them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:16.480
on fire using specialized flint-like teeth. The&nbsp;
blazing quills are an intimidating display, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:23.200
can burn for long periods thanks to Typhlosions’&nbsp;
fire-resistant coat. A really original concept.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:28.000
And the last starter from the Hoenn region is&nbsp;
Feraligatr and its pre-evolutions, rendered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:34.080
in all their reptilian glory. Not surprisingly,&nbsp;
Feraligatr is most analogous to earth alligators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:40.160
or crocodiles. Palmer imagines this lifeform&nbsp;
as a large opportunistic carnivore, lurking in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:46.640
coastal estuaries and frequently traveling between&nbsp;
salt and fresh water habitats. I’d watch my step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:52.000
near rivers with these giants around. Moving&nbsp;
beyond Generation II, we have the incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.600
desert-dwelling Flygon. Palmer envisions that&nbsp;
this swift aerial lifeform possesses a helium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:03.040
bladder that helps the large organism achieve&nbsp;
flight. Flygons skim the surface of the desert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:08.000
looking out from behind a red transparent&nbsp;
membrane which protects their eyes from the sands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.960
And on the ground, near some other interesting&nbsp;
desert fauna I’ll let you classify on your own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:18.000
we can find a Trapinch and a Vibrava — both&nbsp;
reimagined to look like they could more feasibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.080
be early stages in a Flygon’s life cycle. In&nbsp;
this alternative world, millions of years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:30.240
large prehistoric Pokémon stomped across the land,&nbsp;
now known to scientists in the form of fossils.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:36.000
Taking inspiration from the games, Palmer imagines&nbsp;
recent technology has revived these long extinct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.360
species — like the mighty Tyrantrum and the&nbsp;
lumbering Bastiodon: pictured here locked in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:47.040
combat in poses reminiscent of classic dinosaur&nbsp;
art. Not surprisingly, Palmer has based his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.480
Tyrantrum off the T-rex, and has reimagined&nbsp;
its beard as a scaly soft-tissue pouch that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:58.320
could flare out to intimidate rivals. It’s easy to&nbsp;
imagine these creatures living in the primordial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:04.160
landscape of the Pokémon world 100 million years&nbsp;
ago. It almost makes me want to visit a Pokémon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:10.560
natural history museum. Another fossil Pokémon you&nbsp;
might find at such a museum is that of Aerodactyl.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.440
Now resurrected as living creatures, the&nbsp;
Aerodactyl are known by Pokémon paleontologists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.320
to be a vibrant shade of purple, and to&nbsp;
possess early proto-feathers on their necks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:25.680
Elsewhere in this image, one can spot an Archen&nbsp;
and a Cranidos— further fleshing out the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:32.080
of prehistoric Pokémon. Deep in the midst of a&nbsp;
past ice age, the mighty legendary Pokémon Kyurem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:37.280
reigned supreme. Here, Palmer has redesigned&nbsp;
the stylized armor of the original as more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:42.160
biological plating. Though Kyurem was a&nbsp;
creature of almost insurmountable power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:47.920
the legendary beast Raikou — imagined here as a&nbsp;
Smilodon like ice-age hunter — would sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:53.280
take the risk and try to ambush this titan. Though&nbsp;
the bite of a Raikou could take out most Pokémon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:59.200
Palmer imagines it had less effect on Piloswines:&nbsp;
depicted in this image as ice age megafauna with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:04.080
coats so thick they provide a natural defense from&nbsp;
the teeth of whatever else might be stalking the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:10.240
tundra. In modern polar and mountainous biomes,&nbsp;
one might find the elusive Abomasnow — portrayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:16.000
as a curious, heavily furred biped that Palmer&nbsp;
imagines were unknown to Pokémon scientists until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.800
the turn of the century — when a specimen&nbsp;
was at last documented. Sharing the frigid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:26.000
biosphere with these seldom-glimpsed creatures&nbsp;
are the elk-like Pokémon Winter Sawbucks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:31.440
and the penguin-like delibird. And another&nbsp;
lifeform in this image is the infamous Mr. Mime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:37.840
reimagined as a species somewhere between a frog&nbsp;
and an axolotl. And honestly? I find this redesign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:42.720
a bit nicer to look at than the original.&nbsp;
Lurking in the cave systems, one can find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:48.160
Noivern – a species that is a dragon-type in the&nbsp;
games, but that Palmer has reconceptualized as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.680
creature more closely related to bat Pokémon&nbsp;
like Swoobats. A single Noivern can claim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:59.680
miles of cave systems as their territory, scaring&nbsp;
off competitors with booming sonic frequencies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:05.440
Swoobats are their smaller cousins — and look&nbsp;
much closer to a bat here on earth. And if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:11.200
look closely, you can see Joltiks clinging to the&nbsp;
fur of these species. Palmer imagines these small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:17.360
arachnid Pokémon can create static charges by&nbsp;
nestling into the fur of fluffy Pokémon. Pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:24.560
creative! One massive Pokémon Palmer redesigned is&nbsp;
Onix and its evolution Steelix. Instead of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:30.240
made of literal rock, the Onix is depicted as a&nbsp;
massive serpentine lifeform with rock-like scales.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:35.680
Likewise, a Steelix isn’t fully metallic —&nbsp;
instead, exposure to its natural environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:41.360
transforms its scales into shiny, metallic-looking&nbsp;
armor. This process almost reminds me of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:46.960
Scaly-foot Snail — a type of marine gastropod&nbsp;
that incorporates iron sulfide into protective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:52.800
scales on its body — almost like a natural suit&nbsp;
of medieval armor. I could see Steelix perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:58.080
undergoing a similar process to form its own&nbsp;
protective scales. And holding onto dear life at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:04.160
the end of these creatures’ tails, you can spy a&nbsp;
Sableye — reimagined not as a ghost, but as a tiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:10.480
primate with strange eyes similar to a Tarsier.&nbsp;
Palmer describes this species as attracted to tiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:15.840
things, which unfortunately for this Sableye,&nbsp;
included the metallic scales of a Steelix.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:21.600
Under the waves, the aquatic leviathan Gyarados&nbsp;
takes on the role of apex predator in almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:25.920
any aquatic ecosystem they inhabit. This creature&nbsp;
recalls the more biologically feasible design of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:32.640
deep-sea Oarfish, which can grow up to 26ft, or 8m&nbsp;
in length — smaller than this version of Gyarados,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:38.000
but still pretty incredible. Palmer imagines&nbsp;
the marine biologists of this world for decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:44.320
didn’t realize the comparatively tiny Magikarp&nbsp;
were early forms of Gyarados, which makes sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:49.680
Nearby, a greater underwater ecosystem flourishes:&nbsp;
including Cloyster, a squad of Tentacool, and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:55.360
Relicanth — all looking closer to their real-world&nbsp;
animal influences. Returning to legendries, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:01.600
have the awe-inspiring Pokémon Zapdos, an enormous&nbsp;
bird with an incredible electric discharge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:07.040
Instead of being an elemental deity, this version&nbsp;
of Zapdos has an extreme capacity for electric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:12.240
retention — seeking out thunderstorms high in&nbsp;
the atmosphere and gathering electricity like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:20.080
living lightning rod. Another electric Pokémon&nbsp;
is Raichu, the evolution of Pikachu that also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:24.960
looks quite rodent-like. And as you might have&nbsp;
already spotted, this image also contains voltorb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:31.440
reimagined as a pokeball mimicking armadillo, and&nbsp;
Magnemites and Magnetons as magnetic crabs with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:37.680
red and blue colored claws. Truly hilarious&nbsp;
designs for some of the most outside-the-box&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:45.120
original Pokémon. Another ancient and seldom seen&nbsp;
legendary is Lugia, redesigned as a lifeform with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.800
more streamlined, aquatic features. One of my&nbsp;
personal favorites, this guardian of the seas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:55.200
looks like something from the Plesiosauria order —&nbsp;
just even mightier. Near Lugia are some additional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.200 --> 00:09:00.240
underwater species like Qwilfish and Seadra, all&nbsp;
of which have far more anatomical detail than in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:06.320
their original designs. In the skies, the immense&nbsp;
legendary Yveltal — redesigned here as a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:12.640
avian super-predator – casts a shadow over most&nbsp;
flying types when it comes out of hibernation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:17.440
In designing this ancient creature, Palmer took&nbsp;
inspiration from the late Jurassic feathered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:23.120
dinosaur Yi Qi, an animal whose wings are perhaps&nbsp;
the closest thing nature has ever produced to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:29.760
dragon’s. Swooping just out of reach of Yveltal’s&nbsp;
claws are Latios and Latias. With this duo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:35.120
Palmer has merged the stylized airplane-like&nbsp;
elements of the original designs with the anatomy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:40.880
of various real-life bird species — creating more&nbsp;
plausible, but still agile looking lifeforms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:45.920
And in the other corner of this image, you can&nbsp;
spot a scalier-looking Salamence, whose wings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:51.520
according to Palmer, are formed from membranes&nbsp;
extended over an enlarged set of ribs. This might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:56.640
seem far-fetched, but real-life gliding lizards&nbsp;
are capable of gliding flight thanks to a set of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:03.200
wings that are indeed supported by their elongated&nbsp;
ribs, as you can see by this skeleton. And since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:08.480
we covered Groudon in part one, we have to&nbsp;
touch on its oceanic counterpoint Kyogre — which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:14.880
looks awe-inspiring in realistic detail. Palmer&nbsp;
imagines this legendary as a leviathan somewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:21.760
between a whale and the absolute largest of marine&nbsp;
reptiles. To catch prey, Kyogre can use a dazzling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:27.920
bio-luminescent display to lure in smaller&nbsp;
species like Tentacruel. Above the feeding Kyogre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:34.480
a swarm of Frillish and Jellicent drift by, their&nbsp;
anatomy much closer to that of an Earth jellyfish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:39.600
And if you look closely, you might have spotted&nbsp;
Sharpedo — whose stylized body has been reworked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:45.280
with a design that takes inspiration from sharks,&nbsp;
of course, but also Ocean Sunfish — a strange and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:51.200
truncated looking organism. This Sharpedo is going&nbsp;
up against the nautilus-like Omastar — another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:57.120
prehistoric species that was recently revived.&nbsp;
And as our grand finale, we have the mythical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:03.040
and legendary Rayquaza, looking truly epic&nbsp;
in realistic detail. This mighty serpent is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:08.960
exhibiting an unknown display of bioluminescence&nbsp;
as it soars through the air — a sight that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:14.560
be intimidating to behold. Exactly how such&nbsp;
a creature is capable of flight is unknown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:20.240
and would likely require a variety of specialized&nbsp;
flight organs. Believe it or not, there is a type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:26.640
of snake called a Paradise Tree Snake that can&nbsp;
glide by leaping off trees, holding its body flat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:31.200
and curving in a way which allows it to briefly&nbsp;
coast through the air. Rayquaza, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:36.240
is taking this to a new level. And there’s&nbsp;
an entire aviary of flying types flocking in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:42.560
Rayquaza’s shadow — including Pidgeot, Braviary,&nbsp;
and others — all of whom look much more like birds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:49.360
here on Earth. And with that, we’ve covered&nbsp;
the majority of the Realistic Pokémon project.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:53.680
If you want to dive even deeper into this&nbsp;
world, there are extra designs – including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:57.840
some additional legendries — on RJ&nbsp;
Palmer’s site and in his artbooks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:02.480
so check the links below if you’re interested.&nbsp;
For now, this is the final part of the series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:06.480
Thanks for watching. If you enjoyed this entry,&nbsp;
please lend your support and like, subscribe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:23.280
and hit the notification icon to stay up to date&nbsp;
on all things Curious. See you in the next video.

